<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    The entry-reachable Thumb context walk (DIV-154).

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. The pass clears
    it and loads the synthetic UEFI TE fixture through the same bounded TE
    bootstrap the CLI uses: machine ARM (0x1c0), which makes no whole-image
    mode claim, and an AddressOfEntryPoint carrying the Thumb bit. Its .text
    is a Thumb `movs r0,#7; bx lr` at 0x401000 followed by an A32 `bx lr`.

    Without the walk the entry decoded with the language default (A32), so
    `07 20 70 47` was read as a store and a return with no value,
    `void sub_401000(void) { return; }`. The walk follows the flow from the
    entry as Thumb and paints exactly the two instructions it decoded, so the
    function returns 7 while the A32 `bx lr` behind it keeps its own mode.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/te_entrythumbflow_arm.te</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function sub_401000</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="ENTRYTHUMBFLOW #1: the Thumb entry never decodes as A32 returning nothing" min="0" max="0">void sub_401000\(void\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ENTRYTHUMBFLOW #2: the entry is walked as Thumb and returns its constant" min="1" max="1">unsigned int sub_401000\(void\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="ENTRYTHUMBFLOW #3: the constant the Thumb body returns appears exactly once" min="1" max="1">return 7;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
